# ---- HOST-NATIVE STATION (de-bridged 2026-08-16, VICE wave) ----
# VICE 3.10.0's xpet runs ON THE HOST, headless: no QEMU, no guest Debian, no
# X server, no kiosk. Frames VICE_SHM_PATH -> SH_CAPTURE=shm; keys the vicectl
# socket + the ONE shared keysym table (VICE resolves keysym -> matrix through
# PET/gtk3_grus_sym.vkm — the GRAPHICS keyboard, which is this model's, not the
# business one cbm8032 uses); audio -sounddev wav -> named FIFO with the daemon
# as clock. Launcher: the shared stations/vice-native/x11-runtime.sh.
# Build: scripts/build-guests/emulators/build-vice-native.sh pet2001.
#
# ONE BINARY, TWO STATIONS: xpet also serves cbm8032, which is why this station
# has its OWN asset tree and why SH_IDLE_PAUSE_PROC_MATCH below names the path
# and not the binary. -model is the only runtime difference between them.
SH_RESET_MODE=relaunch
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Commodore PET 2001 (1977) power-on screen — "*** COMMODORE BASIC ***", "7167 BYTES FREE" and "READY." in the model's own blueish white on black, drawn double-size (-CRTCdsize) by host-native VICE 3.10.0 xpet -model 2001. PUBLISHED SURFACE 768x432, MEASURED off the shm mapping (1 327 168 B = 64 + 768*432*4) — VICE has no MAME_SHM_SIZE equivalent, the surface IS the emulated screen times CRTCDoubleSize, so this geometry follows from VICE_NATIVE_ARGS and nothing else, and it is NOT the 768x532 SDL window the bridged kiosk sized its X root around. There is no -CRTCborders: VICE's CRTC video code has no border option, so the border comes as the machine draws it. The scene is the machine's own untouched cold boot; nothing is curated and nothing is typed. Keyboard-only exhibit; no pointer device; PET sound -> FIFO.
VICE_NATIVE_BIN=/data/vms/streamhost/assets/pet2001/vice-native/bin/xpet
VICE_NATIVE_DATA=/data/vms/streamhost/assets/pet2001/vice-native/share/vice
# The ONLY thing that decides the published geometry (see SH_FIXTURE_DESC).
VICE_NATIVE_ARGS=-model 2001 -CRTCdsize
SH_VICECTL_SOCK=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/pet2001/ctl.sock
SH_VICESOCK_KEYMAP=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/pet2001/us-layout.keysyms
SH_AUDIO_SOURCE=fifo
SH_AUDIO_FIFO=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/pet2001/audio.fifo

# ---- CHECKPOINT: RESTORED, 2026-08-17 ----
# This station shipped VICE_NATIVE_CHECKPOINT=0 on 2026-08-16 because its
# restore moved the published surface from 768x432 to 768x552. That was an
# UPSTREAM bug, fixed at fork pin c2287e1137:
# crtc_snapshot_read_module() sized the canvas from the SAVED framelines, which
# is the runtime whole-frame line count (260 on this 60 Hz machine) and not the
# nominal 200 every other caller passes — and crtc_update_window() only ever
# grows, so 216 + 60 = 276 native lines stuck. It also resized the viewport
# only when it moved the height itself, while a restore arrives with the height
# right and the viewport wrong.
# Re-measured on the rebuilt binary: restore lands 768x432 with 8222
# non-dominant pixels — the cold-boot gate's own number — and the machine is
# alive at 120 M cycles with a blinking cursor. golden.vsf rebaked 2026-08-17
# against this binary; a checkpoint belongs to ONE machine type and ONE VICE
# build, so a rebuild rebakes.
#
# ---- AND OFF AGAIN, 2026-08-17, FOR A THIRD AND DIFFERENT REASON ----
# THE RESTORE SWAPS THIS MACHINE'S KEYBOARD. Found by the first browser check
# this station ever had: typing `print 12+34` through the real SPA produced
# `4GNHJ]"!Z[$P` — every character wrong, nothing dropped. It is NOT pacing
# (byte-identical garbage at 80/80 and at 60/60) and NOT the keymap file.
#
# The discriminator is vicectl's own banner, which reports how many keysyms the
# machine's .vkm resolved:
#
#   cold boot        keys=149  -> PET/gtk3_grus_sym.vkm, the GRAPHICS keyboard
#                                 this model actually has (151 mappings)
#   after a restore  keys=137  -> PET/gtk3_buuk_sym.vkm, the BUSINESS-UK
#                                 keyboard, which is cbm8032's (139 mappings)
#
# So the restore path reinitialises the PET keyboard TYPE to the family default
# and VICE reloads the business keymap, while the machine underneath is still a
# 2001 with a graphics matrix — so every keysym lands in the wrong cell. Same
# shape as the CRTC canvas-growth bug: a restore leaving a machine resource at
# something other than what the model programs. Reproduced in a namespaced rig
# with the restore as the ONLY variable, and NOT fixed by rebaking: a golden
# freshly baked from a correct `-model 2001` cold boot still comes back 137.
# cbm8032 is immune because business IS its keyboard, which is why the wave
# never saw this.
#
# The exhibit loses NOTHING by cold-booting: this scene IS an untouched cold
# boot, so the checkpoint only ever bought the ~2 s the ROM takes to reach its
# prompt — the same argument the wave used for the four stations that shipped
# with it off. A working keyboard is worth more than 2 s.
# The fork lead is the PET keyboard-type restore in src/pet (petmodel /
# pet-snapshot), and it belongs to whoever owns the fork's restore lane.
VICE_NATIVE_CHECKPOINT=0

# ---- STANDBY (instant-ready: an emulator nobody watches costs ~0 CPU) ----
# The launcher freezes the emulator once the scene has painted; the daemon owns
# it after that (SIGSTOP after the idle grace with zero sessions, unconditional
# SIGCONT the moment a visitor connects). WITHOUT THESE TWO LINES A DE-BRIDGED
# STATION BURNS A FULL CORE AROUND THE CLOCK. _PROC_MATCH is the anti-footgun,
# and on THIS station it is load-bearing rather than defensive: cbm8032 runs a
# process with the same binary NAME, so the path is the only discriminator.
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=60
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_PIDFILE=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/pet2001/vice.pid
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_PROC_MATCH=assets/pet2001/vice-native
VICE_NATIVE_STANDBY_DELAY_S=8

# ---- KEYBOARD PACING ----
# 60/60, the VICE wave's floor, applied fleet-wide on 2026-08-17 — the curve is
# a property of the guest's SCANNED KEYBOARD MATRIX, which every machine in this
# family has, not of the machine's ROM, so the wave moves as one. The wave
# shipped on 80/80 from the vic20 bisect against THIS engine; that bisect fired
# CLEAN press/release sequences, the one shape that reproduces neither of the
# two live key-order defects, and it was RE-RUN with realistic OVERLAPPING
# bursts once the exclusive-scan deadlock was fixed, same framebuffer judge:
# 60/60 clean 12 of 12 at 7.8 keys/s, 40/40 corrupt 6 of 12, 20/20 not applied
# at all. 60/60 is still one clear step above the last pacing OBSERVED to fail
# — the margin that covers a host stall rather than just a frame period — and
# it is 28 % more throughput than 80/80. Verified on this station through the
# REAL SPA in a real browser, not just the control socket.
#
# THIS STATION'S BROWSER CHECK FAILED FIRST TIME, and 60/60 was NOT the cause:
# the corruption was byte-identical at 80/80, which is what proved it was the
# checkpoint restore swapping the keyboard (see the CHECKPOINT block above).
# The pacing stays at the wave's 60/60.
# The launcher hands these to the module as VICE_CTL_KEY_HOLD/_GAP.
SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS=60
SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS=60
# No SH_KEY_MAP and no per-station keymap file: VICE's own symbolic keymap does
# that job.
